[128.588, "o", "\u001b[0;36m> using podman container \u00b7 asking opencode \u00b7 using deepseek-v4-flash-free\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n"]
[131.088, "o", "\u001b[0m\r\n> build \u00b7 deepseek-v4-flash-free\r\n\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[133.588, "o", "\u001b[0m\u2192 \u001b[0mSkill \"bigquery-basics\"\r\n"]
[134.764, "o", "\u001b[0m\u2192 \u001b[0mRead plugin/skills/bigquery-basics/references/core-concepts.md\r\n"]
[136.031, "o", "\u001b[0m\u2731 \u001b[0mGrep \"partition|cluster|PARTITION|CLUSTER\"\u001b[90m in plugin/skills \u00b7 2 matches\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[137.357, "o", "\u001b[0m\u2192 \u001b[0mRead plugin/skills/bigquery-basics/references/iac-usage.md\u001b[90m [offset=1, limit=60]\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[139.857, "o", "Based on the BigQuery skill, here's the optimal design for a 50M-row orders table:\r\n\r\n**Partitioning**\r\n- Partition daily on an `order_ts` TIMESTAMP column (column-based partitioning) \u2014 enables cheap pruning on time-window queries and beyond 90 days rolls into ~50% cheaper long-term storage.\r\n- Avoid ingestion-time partitioning; use the actual order timestamp.\r\n\r\n**Clustering** (1\u20134 keys, adds pruning + better sort)\r\n- `customer_id` (or customer_country), `order_status` \u2014 cluster on the columns you filter/join/aggregate on most, not high-cardinality IDs alone.\r\n- At 50M rows (~tens of GB) clustering is the main lever; daily partitions would be small, so don't cluster on date twice.\r\n\r\n**Schema recommendations**\r\n```json\r\n[\r\n  {\"name\":\"order_id\",\"type\":\"STRING\",\"mode\":\"REQUIRED\"},\r\n  {\"name\":\"customer_id\",\"type\":\"STRING\",\"mode\":\"REQUIRED\"},\r\n  {\"name\":\"order_ts\",\"type\":\"TIMESTAMP\",\"mode\":\"REQUIRED\"},\r\n  {\"name\":\"status\",\"type\":\"STRING\",\"mode\":\"REQUIRED\"},\r\n  {\"name\":\"currency\",\"type\":\"STRING\",\"mode\":\"NULLABLE\"},\r\n  {\"name\":\"amount_total\",\"type\":\"NUMERIC\",\"mode\":\"REQUIRED\"},\r\n  {\"name\":\"shipping_country\",\"type\":\"STRING\",\"mode\":\"NULLABLE\"},\r\n  {\"name\":\"items\",\"type\":\"RECORD\",\"mode\":\"REPEATED\",\r\n    \"fields\":[\r\n      {\"name\":\"sku\",\"type\":\"STRING\",\"mode\":\"REQUIRED\"},\r\n      {\"name\":\"quantity\",\"type\":\"INT64\",\"mode\":\"REQUIRED\"},\r\n      {\"name\":\"unit_price\",\"type\":\"NUMERIC\",\"mode\":\"REQUIRED\"}\r\n    ]}\r\n]\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Create:**\r\n```bash\r\nbq mk --table \\\r\n  --time_partitioning_field=order_ts --time_partitioning_type=DAY \\\r\n  --clustering_fields=customer_id,status \\\r\n  my_dataset.orders schema.json\r\n```\r\n\r\nKey notes: use `NUMERIC` (not FLOAT) for money, `STRING` for IDs (avoids range-partitioning fetch scans), nest line items as `REPEATED RECORD`. For 50M rows this single partitioned+clustered table is simpler and outperforms sharded tables. Trade-off: clustering `customer_id` optimizes per-user lookups but adds insert cost \u2014 if your workload is regional reporting instead, cluster on `shipping_country,status` instead.\r\n"]
